        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is CVS Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, in a limited sense: CVS Health Whey goes through CVS's Tested to Be Trusted program, but it does not carry an independent certification seal such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Those are two different things, and the difference matters most to athletes who are drug-tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            CVS launched Tested to Be Trusted in 2019 after starting testing in 2017. Under the program, every product with a supplement facts panel that CVS sells, its own brand and name brands alike, must either hold an NSF certification or USP verification, or pass third-party testing arranged through CVS. Coverage of the launch reported that suppliers must have third-party testing on every ingredient and dose listed on the facts panel, plus contaminant testing for things such as pesticides and heavy metals, and must meet good manufacturing practice standards. CVS said about 7% of the products it tested failed at launch and were pulled or relabeled.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a real filter, and it is more than most store brands publish. Walmart's Equate whey, for comparison, carries no testing claim on its listing. But it has limits. CVS does not publish test results for individual products or lots, so you cannot look up CVS Health Whey and see a lead figure, a certificate number or a test date. And the program checks label accuracy and contaminants; it is not a banned-substance screen designed for sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete in a tested sport, buy a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases and check the exact flavor and lot. Of the brands on CVS shelves, some Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard flavors and Muscle Milk products are listed in NSF's sport registry, and Body Fortress appears in NSF's general supplement registry under NSF/ANSI 173, which checks contents and contaminants. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> and <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> go through each listing.
          </p>

