        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does CVS Protein Powder Cost per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            CVS Health Whey costs about $1.50 per 25 g of protein at its $36.39 shelf price, and about $1.20 per 25 g when CVS runs it at the $29.11 promotional price we saw. Those figures use the 39 g scoop on the label, which works out to about 23 servings and 600 g of protein in a 32 oz tub. CVS's online listings did not show a servings count, so check the panel; if your tub lists more servings, the cost per gram falls.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math is simple once you have the label. Divide the price by the total grams of protein in the tub, then multiply by 25. For CVS Health Whey at full price: 23 servings times 26 g is about 600 g of protein, or 24 portions of 25 g, and $36.39 divided by 24 is about $1.50. Run the same sum on any tub on the shelf and you can compare a 2 lb whey with a 1 lb plant powder fairly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against the rest of the store-brand world, CVS is not the bargain. Walmart's <a href="/equate-protein-powder/">Equate whey</a> costs about $0.94 per 25 g at Walmart's price, and Costco's Kirkland and Aldi's Elevation are similarly low. CVS's price sits closer to name brands bought online: Premier Protein at about $1.47 per 25 g and Gold Standard at about $1.43 in the 5 lb tub on Amazon at the prices we saw on November 20, 2026.
          </p>

          <p>
            Name brands at CVS cost more than the same tubs online. CVS listed Premier Protein whey powder at $42.49 for a 24.5 oz tub. At about 17 servings of 30 g, that is close to $2.08 per 25 g, against $1.47 for the same brand on Amazon. CVS's buy-one-get-one-40%-off and ExtraCare deals close part of that gap, so if you buy at CVS, buy two tubs on a promotion rather than one at full price.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never pay CVS shelf price for a name-brand tub.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The same Premier Protein powder cost about 40% more per gram at CVS's listed price than on Amazon when we checked. CVS Health Whey on a buy-one-get-one deal is the only CVS protein powder that is competitive on price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="name-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Name-Brand Protein Powders Does CVS Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            CVS stocks about a dozen name-brand protein powder lines, led by Premier Protein, Optimum Nutrition, Body Fortress, Six Star and Muscle Milk on the whey side and Orgain, Vega, OWYN, Garden of Life and Vital Proteins on the plant side. CVS.com's brand filter showed several Optimum Nutrition and Muscle Milk powders when we checked, though most stores carry only a selection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whey powders at CVS</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Premier Protein 100% Whey</strong> gives 30 g of protein per scoop and is the familiar name for anyone who drinks the shakes; our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein review</a> covers it in full. <strong>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</strong> is the benchmark whey, sold at CVS in a 1.5 lb tub. <strong>Body Fortress Super Advanced Whey</strong> and <strong>Six Star Elite Series Whey</strong> are the budget-brand tubs, with Six Star sold at CVS in a 32 oz size; see our <a href="/body-fortress-protein-powder-review/">Body Fortress review</a> and today's <a href="/six-star-protein-powder-review/">Six Star review</a>. <strong>Isopure Zero Carb</strong> is the pure isolate option, in a 1 lb tub.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plant powders at CVS</h3>

          <p>
            CVS's plant shelf is stronger than many grocery stores'. <strong>Orgain Organic</strong> gives 21 g of protein per serving and is USDA Organic, per Orgain. <strong>Vega Original</strong> lists 25 g per two scoops, <strong>OWYN</strong> lists 30 g from pea and pumpkin seed protein, and <strong>Garden of Life Organic Protein + Greens</strong> and <strong>Vital Proteins Plant Protein</strong> each list 20 g. Our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain</a>, <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega</a> and <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">OWYN</a> reviews break down each label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because all of these carry a supplement facts panel or are sold in the supplement aisle, CVS says they fall under the same Tested to Be Trusted requirement as its own brand. In practice that means the name brands at CVS have either their own certification or CVS's screen, which is a point in the chain's favor if you value a retailer filter. For head-to-head label comparisons between any two of these products, our <a href="/compare/">protein powder comparison tool</a> lines them up by protein, calories and cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-it-good" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is CVS Protein Powder Good?
          </h2>

          <p>
            CVS protein powder is good enough for everyday use but not the best value: CVS Health Whey delivers a solid 26 g of protein per scoop, passes CVS's testing program and is easy to find, but it costs more per gram than Walmart's or Costco's store brands and offers one flavor. For most people it is a convenience buy rather than a first choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it does well is the basics. A concentrate-and-isolate blend is the same protein type as most mainstream wheys, it provides all the essential amino acids, and 26 g per scoop clears the roughly 20 to 40 g that research suggests for a post-workout or between-meal dose for most adults. At about 150 calories a scoop it is a lean shake, not a mass gainer, so it fits weight-loss and maintenance goals as well as muscle building.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it falls short is price, choice and transparency. At full price it costs roughly 60% more per gram than Equate whey, and the single vanilla flavor leaves no room if you dislike it. The label also carries maltodextrin, added sugar, two artificial sweeteners and soy lecithin, so it is a less clean recipe than the shortest-label wheys, and CVS does not show a servings count online.
          </p>

          <p>
            So is it good? For a recreational lifter or anyone adding protein to their day, yes, especially on a buy-one-get-one deal. For drug-tested athletes, people chasing the lowest price per gram, or anyone who wants flavor variety, a name brand from our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder</a> list or the Amazon alternatives above are better buys. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take prescription medication or have a milk allergy, check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About CVS Whey's Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is little written buyer feedback on CVS Health Whey that we could read, so we cannot give a reliable picture of its taste. CVS.com shows a star rating on the product page, but the page blocks automated readers and we could not open the written reviews, and the tub is not sold on Amazon, where most protein reviews pile up. We have not tasted it ourselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the label does tell you is how it is likely to behave. Maltodextrin, cellulose gum, xanthan gum and gum arabic are thickeners, and lipolyzed cream adds a dairy richness, so expect a thicker, creamier vanilla shake than a plain whey such as Equate or Gold Standard. Soy lecithin is there to help it mix in a shaker. Two artificial sweeteners plus a little sugar usually mean a sweeter taste, which some people find pleasant and others call artificial.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints that come up most often for budget vanilla wheys in general are a sweetener aftertaste, small clumps in water, and bloating for people sensitive to whey concentrate. We cannot confirm how often CVS buyers report these, so check the newest reviews on CVS.com before you buy a full tub. If you are unsure, a blender, milk or a smoothie base hides most texture and flavor issues with any vanilla whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            If taste is your priority, a name brand gives you far more evidence and more choice. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard and Premier Protein each come in many flavors with thousands of buyer reviews, and our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide ranks flavors by buyer feedback across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Should You Buy Protein Powder at CVS or Online?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy protein powder at CVS when you need it today or when CVS Health Whey is on a buy-one-get-one deal; buy it online the rest of the time. The same name-brand tubs cost noticeably more at CVS's listed prices than on Amazon, and CVS's own whey only beats them on promotion.
          </p>

          <p>
            CVS makes sense for a few kinds of shoppers. If you are already at the pharmacy, ran out of protein and want something reasonable in your bag tonight, CVS Health Whey or a small Premier tub does the job. If you value a retailer testing filter and like the idea that every supplement on the shelf has passed CVS's screen, CVS is one of the few chains that publishes such a policy. And ExtraCare rewards and coupons can bring the price down for regular CVS shoppers.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the numbers favor buying online or at a warehouse store. Gold Standard in the 5 lb size works out to about $1.43 per 25 g on Amazon at the price we saw, Premier Protein about $1.47, and Walmart's Equate under $1. Our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">Walmart</a>, <a href="/best-target-protein-powder/">Target</a> and <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">Costco</a> guides compare those stores aisle by aisle, and our <a href="/sprouts-protein-powder/">Sprouts</a> and <a href="/gnc-protein-powder-review/">GNC</a> pages cover two more store options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever store you choose, compare on cost per 25 g of protein, not tub price, and read the facts panel for the scoop size and servings. That one habit tells you more than any shelf tag, and it is how we rank every product on this site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Tested to Be Trusted Means Sport Certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CVS's program checks label accuracy and screens for contaminants such as heavy metals, but it is not a banned-substance certification. If you are drug-tested, CVS Health Whey is not the product to rely on. Look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport and verify the exact flavor in the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Full Price for Name Brands at CVS</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier Protein powder at CVS's $42.49 listing costs about $2.08 per 25 g, against about $1.47 for the same brand on Amazon. That gap repeats across most name brands on the CVS shelf. Buy at CVS on a promotion or when you need a tub today, not as your regular source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Online Listing Over the Tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party listings for CVS Health Whey disagree on scoop size, sugar and sodium, and CVS does not show a servings count online. Read the supplement facts panel on the tub you are buying. Scoop size and servings decide the cost per gram, and the sugar line matters if you are watching carbs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Tub Prices Instead of Protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1 lb plant tub and a 2 lb whey tub can look similarly priced but hold very different amounts of protein. Divide the price by total grams of protein and multiply by 25. That is the only fair way to compare CVS Health Whey with Premier, Gold Standard or Orgain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Whey When You Need Dairy-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CVS Health Whey is milk-based and its label declares soy too, so it is out for anyone with a milk allergy or a vegan diet. CVS has no store-brand plant powder. Pick Orgain, Vega or OWYN from the same aisle, and check allergen statements before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CVS protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Partly. CVS Health Whey falls under CVS's Tested to Be Trusted program, which requires third-party testing of the ingredients and doses on a supplement's facts panel plus contaminant screening for things like heavy metals. It does not carry NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and CVS publishes no product-level results, so drug-tested athletes should choose a certified brand.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CVS protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is good for everyday use but not the best value. CVS Health Whey gives 26 g of protein and 150 calories per 39 g scoop, per the label, and passes CVS's testing screen. At roughly $1.50 per 25 g at full price it costs more than Walmart's Equate or Costco's Kirkland whey, so it is best bought on a buy-one-get-one promotion.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CVS Health Whey healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a reasonable everyday protein but not a clean-label one. Per the label, a scoop has 150 calories, 2.5 g of fat and 2 g of sugar, but the ingredients include maltodextrin, sugar, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, soy lecithin and three gums. It is not organic or certified clean. People with kidney disease or other medical conditions should ask a doctor first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does CVS protein powder contain lead or heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  CVS does not publish heavy-metal results for its whey. Its Tested to Be Trusted program requires contaminant testing, including metals, before products can be sold, and CVS said about 7% of products failed its initial screening. That gives some reassurance, but without published figures you cannot compare CVS's lead levels with independently tested brands.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does CVS have a plant-based protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not under its own brand, as far as we could find. CVS Health's only current protein powder we could confirm is its whey. For dairy-free protein, CVS stocks name brands including Orgain Organic, Vega, OWYN, Garden of Life and Vital Proteins plant powders, which list between 20 g and 30 g of protein per serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What flavor of CVS protein powder is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Vanilla is the only CVS Health Whey flavor we could confirm on CVS.com, so there is little to choose between. Nutrition databases also list a chocolate version, but we could not find it for sale. If flavor matters, name brands like Premier Protein and Gold Standard offer many more options, with far more buyer reviews to judge them by.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about CVS protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not read enough written CVS reviews to summarize them reliably. The clear drawbacks are on the label and price tag: about $1.50 per 25 g at full price, a single flavor, 7 g of carbohydrate and two artificial sweeteners. Typical complaints about budget vanilla wheys, such as aftertaste and bloating, may apply; check recent CVS.com reviews.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

